|-
| 10000 || 1.40 || 0.845 || ~2 cm || Defender
|}

Note: because ferrofluid channels occupy only ~4% of composite volume, the composite μ_ψ changes modestly (1.00–1.40 × μ₀) across the full Langevin curve. The coherence length tuning range for the composite is approximately 1.00–0.85, i.e., roughly a 15% contraction from the H=0 baseline. The absolute L_ψ values depend on L_ψ^vac, which is a TML-1 calibration quantity.

=== Lever 2 — BST Bias Voltage (Secondary, via ε_ψ) ===

From [[ψ Permittivity]] (B2), a 200V BST bias reduces <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> by ~10%:

:<math>\frac{L_\psi(200\,\text{V})}{L_\psi(0\,\text{V})} = \sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_\psi(0\,\text{V})}{\varepsilon_\psi(200\,\text{V})}} \approx \sqrt{\frac{4.0}{3.6}} \approx 1.054</math>

BST voltage increases L_ψ by ~5% at 200V bias. This is a smaller and opposite effect to the H-field lever — the BST bias slightly extends coherence length while the H-field contracts it. In the Mk2.0 servo, both levers are used simultaneously for fine-grained L_ψ control:

* Coarse adjustment: ferrofluid H-field (large range, slow settling ~100 ms for ferrofluid redistribution)
* Fine adjustment: BST bias voltage (small range ±5%, fast settling < 1 ms)

=== Lever 3 — Composite Formulation (Fixed at Fabrication) ===

The choice of composite phases and their volume fractions sets the baseline <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> (at H=0, 0V bias) and therefore the baseline L_ψ before any real-time tuning. This is a design choice made at the [[TPMS Lattice Design]] (B7) and [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8) stage and cannot be changed during operation.

== The Nonlinear Coherence Length Shift ==

From [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] (A3), the vacuum effective mass depends on background ψ field:

:<math>m_{\rm eff,vac}^2 = m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2</math>

When the operator sustains a background ψ field <math>\psi_0</math> (through coherent neural firing during Anchor mode), their biological coherence length contracts:

:<math>L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}(\psi_0) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{(m^2 + 3\lambda\psi_0^2)\,\varepsilon_\psi^{\rm bio}\mu_\psi^{\rm bio}}}</math>

The Mk2.0 [[Anchor Controller]] must match the device coherence length to the operator's biological coherence length for optimal impedance coupling (from [[ψ Impedance Theory]] A4). As the operator's coherence increases during an Anchor session, <math>\psi_0</math> increases, <math>m_{\rm eff}^{\rm (bio)}</math> increases, and <math>L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> decreases. The controller must respond by increasing the ferrofluid H-field set-point (increasing <math>\mu_\psi</math>, decreasing <math>L_\psi^{\rm (device)}</math>) to track the shrinking biological coherence length.

The RMSSD HRV metric is the proxy for this tracking: increasing RMSSD ↔ increasing <math>\psi_0</math> ↔ decreasing <math>L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> ↔ required increase in ferrofluid H-field.

== Analogues from Established Physics ==

L_ψ belongs to a family of penetration lengths that appear across condensed matter and field theory:

